  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Thursday)\nIssued at 211 PM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nCurrent upper air models and Water Vapor show ridge axis over the\nlee side of the rockies, with trough over the Great Lakes, and \nnorthwest flow over the region. 21Z surface analysis shows high \npressure over southeastern North Dakota this afternoon. Satellite \nshows lingering fog/stratus stretching from northwest into south \ncentral SD. Winds are currently light with temperatures ranging \nfrom the upper 30s to near 60.\n\nThe fog and low stratus will continue to burn off this afternoon,\nbut is expected to redevelop tonight and continue into Friday\nmorning. Main areas for fog are expected in and around the\nfoothills, but will likely see some patchy fog and stratus over\nthe western SD plains as well. Decent mixing on Friday will bring\ngusty southeast winds across the region, with the highest gusts\n(30-40mph) over the plains areas. Similar setup continues on\nSaturday. Little change with the temperature forecast over the \nweekend as the overall ridging pattern holds through the weekend.\nModels show a weak (but dry) shortwave passing through towards the\nend of the weekend, and we'll see the pattern start to change a\nbit early next week as the ridge shifts eastward and flattens\nsomewhat. Next chance for any meaningful precipitation returns\nmiddle of next week, as long range models show upper trough\nswinging up from the four corners region into the northern plains.\nThe GFS and ECMWF differ a bit on placement and we're still a \nweek out, so low confidence on location, amount and p-type we \nmight see from this disturbance.",
